    <title>Gin in England: Social Impact</title>
    <content>
<![CDATA[
<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<!DOCTYPE en-note SYSTEM 'http://xml.evernote.com/pub/enml2.dtd'>
<en-note>
<h2>Gin in England and social harm</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Gin originated in the Netherlands and was introduced to England by soldiers returning from the Low Countries.</li>
  <li>In the 18th century, cheap gin consumption became a major social problem in England.</li>
</ul>
<p>Hogarth's 1751 print <i>Gin Lane</i> includes the inscription:</p>
<blockquote>"Drunk for a Penny / Dead Drunk for two pence / Clean Straw for Nothing"</blockquote>
<p>This line underscores how cheap gin fueled harmful social conditions.</p>
<p>References: <a href="https://www.britannica.com/topic/gin-liquor">Britannica: Gin</a>; <a href="https://www.royalacademy.org.uk/art-artists/work-of-art/gin-lane-1">Royal Academy of Arts: Gin Lane</a></p>
<p>Attachment (PDF):</p>
<en-media type="application/pdf" hash="62cc3e0bda8a8f8353caf1303369e933"/>
</en-note>
